<Base title="HIPAA Notice of Privacy Practices" counterpart="/zh/hipaa-notice/" description="How health information may be used and disclosed and how to exercise your privacy rights."><article class="legal-page"><p class="kicker">Privacy</p><h1>HIPAA Notice of Privacy Practices</h1><p class="lede"><strong>This notice describes how medical information about you may be used and disclosed and how you can get access to this information. Please review it carefully.</strong></p><aside class="legal-alert"><strong>Legal and compliance review required before publication.</strong> Reminiscence Cloud must insert its exact legal entity name, privacy contact, phone, email, effective date, state-specific provisions for New York, New Jersey, and California, and determine whether 42 CFR Part 2 applies. This web draft does not replace the practice’s signed, current Notice of Privacy Practices.</aside><h2>Your rights</h2><p>You have the right to:</p><ul><li>Get an electronic or paper copy of your medical record and other health information, subject to lawful limits.</li><li>Ask to correct health information you believe is incorrect or incomplete.</li><li>Ask for confidential communications in a particular way or at a particular location.</li><li>Ask the practice to limit certain uses or disclosures. The practice is not always required to agree, except in circumstances required by law.</li><li>Get an accounting of certain disclosures of your health information.</li><li>Get a paper copy of this notice, even if you agreed to receive it electronically.</li><li>Choose someone with lawful authority to act for you.</li><li>File a complaint without retaliation if you believe your privacy rights were violated.</li></ul><h2>Your choices</h2><p>For certain information, you may tell the practice your preferences about sharing information with family, friends, or others involved in your care; disaster-relief situations; fundraising; and other uses for which the law gives you a choice. Uses and disclosures for marketing, sale of health information, and most uses of psychotherapy notes generally require written authorization when applicable.</p><h2>How health information may be used and shared</h2><p>As permitted or required by law, the practice may use or disclose protected health information to:</p><ul><li>Provide, coordinate, or manage treatment and consult with other health-care professionals.</li><li>Bill for services and obtain payment.</li><li>Operate the practice, improve care, train staff, conduct quality activities, and perform necessary business functions.</li><li>Help with public-health and safety activities, report suspected abuse or neglect, prevent or reduce a serious threat, comply with health oversight, respond to lawful legal process, support workers’ compensation, work with medical examiners or funeral directors, and comply with other legal requirements.</li><li>Contact you about appointments, care coordination, treatment alternatives, or health-related services when permitted.</li></ul><p>Uses or disclosures not described in the notice and not otherwise permitted by law generally require your written authorization. You may revoke an authorization in writing, except to the extent action has already been taken in reliance on it.</p><h2>Our responsibilities</h2><ul><li>We are required by law to maintain the privacy and security of protected health information.</li><li>We will notify you promptly if a breach occurs that may have compromised the privacy or security of your information, as required by law.</li><li>We must follow the duties and privacy practices described in the notice currently in effect and make a copy available to you.</li><li>We will not use or share your information other than as described in the notice unless you authorize it in writing, except as otherwise permitted or required by law.</li></ul><h2>Substance use disorder records and state law</h2><p>Federal rules governing certain substance use disorder patient records under 42 CFR Part 2 may impose additional protections. More protective state laws may also apply. Before publication, the practice must determine whether Part 2 applies and add the provisions required by the laws of each state in which it provides services.</p><h2>Changes to this notice</h2><p>The practice may change the terms of its notice, and lawful changes may apply to health information already maintained. A current notice will be available upon request, in the office when applicable, and on this website.</p><h2>Questions or complaints</h2><p>Questions or complaints may be directed to the practice’s designated privacy contact. <strong>Privacy contact name, email, telephone number, and mailing address must be inserted before publication.</strong></p><p>You may also file a complaint with the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services Office for Civil Rights. The practice will not retaliate against you for filing a complaint. See <a href="https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/filing-a-complaint/">HHS instructions for filing a complaint</a>.</p><h2>Effective date</h2><p><strong>An effective date must be inserted after final legal and compliance approval.</strong></p><h2>Authoritative review source</h2><p>This draft follows the structure of the <a href="https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/guidance/privacy-practices-health-care-provider/">February 2026 HHS Model Notice of Privacy Practices for HIPAA Covered Health Care Providers</a>. The practice should compare its final notice against the current model and its actual privacy practices before adoption.</p></article></Base>
